// Local-development config, and only that. The `*:local:push` tasks pass it with
// an explicit --config; nothing else reads it.
//
// The deliberate part is the filename. Wrangler auto-loads wrangler.toml /
// wrangler.json / wrangler.jsonc, so a config under any of those names would also
// apply to `wrangler pages deploy` in staging.yml and release.yml — which take
// their project name on the command line and get their real D1 binding from the
// Pages project itself (declared in infra's terraform). This name is invisible to
// them.
//
// It exists because `wrangler d1 execute` resolves a database by binding or name
// from a config file and has no way to be handed one on the command line.
// `wrangler pages dev` is the opposite — it refuses --config outright and wants
// --d1 BINDING=ID — so `task dev` passes the flag instead of reading this.
//
// The one thing that has to line up is the id: --local keys its sqlite file under
// .wrangler/ by database_id, so `task dev`'s --d1 ANSWERS=<id> and the id below
// must match or the seeded rows land in a database the dev server never opens.
{
  "name": "guessr-local",
  "pages_build_output_dir": "web",
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "ANSWERS",
      "database_name": "guessr-answers-local",
      // Not a real Cloudflare database id — --local never talks to Cloudflare.
      // The deployed databases are bound by terraform, not from here.
      "database_id": "guessr-answers-local"
    }
  ]
}
